# contents_index.xlsx -- FROZEN EXPECTATION.
# A frozen expectation is corrected ONLY when the reading of the third-party-authored fixture was
# wrong -- never edited to chase an FSA1 regression.
#
# The `region:` lines are the SEMANTIC reading, written from the authored structure before `unpack`
# was run on this file. The `block:` lines are the hand-derivation below, executed on the fixture's
# own `s=` attributes.
#
# THE SIGNATURES, off xl/worksheets/sheet1.xml:
#   s="1" on A1:B3     the chapter entries, flush left            -- x
#   s="2" on A4:B6     the subsection entries, indented two       -- y
# The two xf entries state the same font, the same fill, the same border and the same General number
# format. They differ in their `alignment` child and in nothing else, which is the whole fixture: an
# xf carries alignment, so an author's indent is a signature change and the index is two regions.
# Every one of the twelve coordinates is occupied, and there is no blank row between the halves.
#
# 1. SEED. x is the six cells of rows 1-3: two wide, three deep -> A1:B3. y -> A4:B6. Each is gapless
#    over one signature, so each costs one rule: the potential is 2 + 2 lambda = 4.
#
# 2. PASS 1, parity 0: A1:B3.
#    Down it lands on row 4 and takes A4:B4 across both its columns, cutting A4:B6 to A5:B6. The
#    merged A1:B4 is gapless: modal x at six cells, one row rule for the y row -> 2. Before 1+1+1+1 =
#    4, after 2+1 plus 1+1 = 5 -> -1. Row 0 is above the sheet and columns C..E hold nothing, so that
#    is its only reachable move. Nothing applied.
#
# 3. PASS 2, parity 1: A4:B6.
#    Up it takes A3:B3, cutting A1:B3 to A1:B2. The merged A3:B6 is gapless: modal y at six cells, one
#    row rule for the x row -> 2. Before 4, after 2+1 plus 1+1 = 5 -> -1. Rows 7-9 are empty, so that
#    is its only reachable move. Nothing applied, and the pass fixed the second parity: growth stops.
#
#    Neither half swallows the other because BOTH are three rows deep. A merge of two stacked,
#    same-width, one-signature regions costs one row rule per row of the smaller one and refunds one
#    lambda, so it pays while one side is a single row and stops paying at two. TWO blocks.
#
# 4. `--decompose occupancy` reads no signature: 12 occupied cells in a 2x6 bounding box, area 12,
#    under the policy's 256-cell floor -- and there is no empty row or column to cut at in any case --
#    so it writes ONE block A1:B6. Of the two labelled starts -- rows 1 and 4 -- it emits row 1 and
#    misses one.
sheet:  Contents
region: 1-3 the chapter entries
region: 4-6 the subsection entries under Results
block:  A1:B3
block:  A4:B6
policy: appearance
misses: 1
